# Security baseline

## Secrets
- Never put secrets, API keys, tokens, passwords or private keys in code, tests, CI configuration, commit messages or logs.
- Read credentials from the environment or the CI secret store at runtime. If a change needs a new secret, name the variable and stop; do not invent placeholder values that look real.

## External input
- Treat every byte from the network, files, environment variables and command-line arguments as untrusted.
- Validate length, range and format before use. Reject malformed input with an explicit error instead of guessing or repairing it.

## Network calls and dependencies
- Do not add network calls (sockets, HTTP clients, DNS lookups, outbound connections from scripts or CI) without review.
  - Agent shells deny curl, wget and netcat, and inline scripts that make network calls: `python -c` code that uses urllib, requests or socket; `node -e` / `--eval` / `-p` / `--print`, `bun -e` / `--eval` and `deno eval` code that uses fetch, http, https, net, tls, dgram or undici; `perl -e` / `-E` and `ruby -e` code (or `-M` / `-r` modules) that uses LWP, HTTP::Tiny, IO::Socket, Net::HTTP, Net::FTP, open-uri, Socket or URI; and inline code of any of these that names curl, wget or netcat.
- Do not add libraries, packages, toolchains, GitHub Actions or downloaded binaries without review. Prefer what the distribution's package manager already provides.
- New third-party code always enters this repository through a human-reviewed pull request, never from an agent shell.
- New outside code (dependencies, repositories, remotes) needs human review before it enters this repository. Agent shells enforce this in `.cursor/hooks/shell_policy.py` and deny:
  - `git clone`, `git submodule add`, `git remote add` and `git archive --remote` (any remote, including `origin`), always.
  - `git ls-remote` against anything other than `origin`; bare `git ls-remote` and `git ls-remote origin` (with `--heads` or `--tags`) are allowed.
  - `git fetch`, `git pull` and `git remote update` from any remote other than `origin`: another remote name, a URL (`https://`, `git@`, `ssh://`, `file://`) or a local path, and `--all`. With no remote or with `origin` they are allowed, also after `git -C <dir>` and harmless `-c key=value` such as `core.quotepath=false` or `protocol.version=2`.
  - Any git command whose config is overridden so that `origin` could point elsewhere: `-c` or `--config-env` on a `remote.*`, `url.*` (`insteadOf`, `pushInsteadOf`), `include.*` or `includeIf.*` key; `GIT_CONFIG_KEY_<n>` or `GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS` naming such a key; and a moved config file (`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, `GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM`, `GIT_CONFIG`, or `HOME` / `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` for git). This covers `VAR=... git`, `env VAR=... git`, nested `bash -c`, `export`, and standalone assignments; the `GIT_CONFIG_*` variables are denied in front of any command.
  - Retargeting remotes: `git remote set-url` (including `--add` and `--push`), `git remote rename` onto `origin`, `git config` writes (any file option, `--add`, `--replace-all`, `git config set`) to `remote.*.url`, `remote.*.pushurl`, `url.*.insteadOf`, `url.*.pushInsteadOf`, `include.path` or `includeIf.*`, section renames to or from `remote`, `url` or include sections, and `git config --edit`. Reads such as `git config --get remote.origin.url`, `git config -l` and `git remote get-url origin` are allowed.
  - Package installs: `pip install` (also `python -m pip install`), `npm install` / `npm i` / `npm add`, `yarn add`, `pnpm add`, `go get` and `cargo add`, and `pnpm install` / `pnpm i` / `yarn install` with a package name. `npm ci`, `npm test`, `go build`, `cargo build`, and `pnpm install` / `yarn install` without a package name are allowed.
  - GitHub CLI: `gh repo clone`, `gh gist clone`, `gh release download` (any repository, including this one), `gh extension install` and `gh repo fork --clone` / `--remote`, always. Any `gh` command aimed at a repository other than `quinndarling21/sigrelay` through `-R` / `--repo` (`OWNER/REPO`, `HOST/OWNER/REPO` or a URL) or `GH_REPO`, and any `gh` command with `GH_HOST` other than `github.com`. `gh pr checkout` / `gh pr diff` given another repository's URL. `gh api` on a `repos/<owner>/<repo>` path of another repository (with or without a leading `/`, or as an `https://api.github.com` URL), on `repositories/<id>`, on another host (`--hostname` or a URL), and `gh api graphql` that names another owner or repository, reads its query from a file, or looks up `node`, `nodes` or `search`. Everything against this repository is allowed: `gh pr ...`, `gh run list` / `view` / `download`, `gh release list` / `view`, and `gh api repos/quinndarling21/sigrelay/...` or `repos/{owner}/{repo}/...`, also with `-R quinndarling21/sigrelay`.
- When an agent shell blocks a command, do not work around it; report what you wanted to add and why.

## Logging
- Use structured logging with explicit levels (error, warn, info, debug). Every message carries an event name and key-value fields, not free-form string concatenation.
- Never log full SIP or Diameter message payloads.
- Never log subscriber identifiers (IMSI, MSISDN, SIP URIs or anything derived from them) at info level or above. When an identifier is needed for debugging, log it at debug level only, truncated or hashed.
- Never log secrets, session keys or authentication material at any level.
